== history ==
The year this shrine was founded is unknown, but it is also mentioned in [[Engishiki|the Engishiki]], which was created during [[Heian period|the Heian period]] . In the newly compiled surname register compiled in the early Heian period, there is a description that ``Yagizō, son of Wadasin Toyotama no Mikoto, after Furutama no Mikoto,<nowiki>''</nowiki> indicates that the Yagi clan, an ancient powerful clan, enshrined their ancestral deity, Furutama no Mikoto. It is said that it originated from {{Sfn|岸和田市観光振興協会|1993}} . It used to be the village shrine of Yagigo, but when village shrines were established in each village during [[Edo period|the Edo period]], it was revered as {{Sfn|岸和田市観光振興協会|1993}} village shrine of Nakai Village. is said to be a corruption of ``Nakayagi<nowiki>''</nowiki> {{Sfn|大阪府神道青年会|1971}} ). Afterwards, in [[1872]] ( [[Meiji period|Meiji]] 5) in the Meiji era, it was ranked as a village shrine, and from [[1908]] to [[1909|1909,]] Yagi village, Kitakashimori village Oaza Yoshii, [[Tadaoka, Osaka|Tadaoka village]] Oaza Kitade・The Takatsuki Village Shrine was enshrined {{Sfn|岸和田市観光振興協会|1993}} . It was promoted to Gosha in [[1943]] ( [[Shōwa period|Showa]] 18). {{Sfn|大阪府神道青年会|1971}}

