== history == The year this shrine was founded is unknown, but it is also mentioned in [[Engishiki|the Engishiki]], which was created during [[Heian period|the Heian period]] . In the {{ill|Shinsen Shōjiroku|en}} compiled in the early Heian period, there is a description indicates that the Yagi clan, an ancient powerful clan, enshrined their ancestral deity, {{ill|Furutama-no-mikoto|ja|布留多摩命|6=simple|7=User:Immanuelle/Furutama|8=en|9=Draft:Furutama-no-mikoto}}. {{Sfn|岸和田市観光振興協会|1993}} . Several shrines were merged with it during the [[Meiji period]] <ref name=":0" />{{Sfn|岸和田市観光振興協会|1993}} . It was promoted to Gosha in [[1943]] ( [[Shōwa period|Showa]] 18). {{Sfn|大阪府神道青年会|1971}}
== cultural assets ==
* Kishiwada City designated tangible cultural property ** Ema for rain (by Tsuda Unkei) <ref name="kishiwada">{{Cite web|date=2016-06-02|title=岸和田市内の指定文化財一覧 - 岸和田市公式ウェブサイト|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160602015330/https://www.city.kishiwada.osaka.jp/soshiki/70/sitei-bunkazai2015.html|access-date=2024-03-23|website=web.archive.org}}</ref> * Kishiwada City designated natural monument ** Yagi Shrine shrine complex <ref name="kishiwada"></ref>
== Festivals ==
* Regular festival: [[October 5|October 5th]] every year * [[Matsuri float|Danjiri]] shrine procession: Two days before {{ill|Sports Day (Japan)|lt=Sports Day|en}} every year {{Sfn|岸和田市観光振興協会|1993}}
